xnu 0386: /dev/cu.bluetooth, the HCI transport bluetoothd opens

On iOS 16.7.16 bluetoothd's primary transport is an IOKit
AppleBluetoothModule service, which this kernel does not have, and its
fallback is a serial port: it calls access("/dev/cu.bluetooth"), then
access("/dev/uart.bluetooth"), and logs "No valid serial port found (tried
cu.bluetooth and uart.bluetooth)" when both are missing. The binary imports
tcgetattr, tcsetattr, cfsetspeed and cfmakeraw, so the node must be a tty:
a plain cdev would fail the termios conversation before the first HCI packet.

The node therefore exists from boot, because bluetoothd is started by launchd
long before a ring command could create anything: `mondrian_bt_tty_init()` runs
in the root-mount probe block. The part is powered and the wire programmed on
the first open, which is the first moment the chip has to be alive, and that
reuses the measured `btuart on` sequence (pins, 14745600 Hz root, 8N1) instead
of a second copy of it; the SE root is changed **once** per boot, which is the
constraint 0343 measured.

Input is drained into the line discipline from a one-tick timeout instead of an
interrupt: SPI 587 has no handler in this kernel, and enabling a level line
nothing acknowledges is what 0343 avoids. One tick is 10 ms, 115 bytes at
115200, below the engine's 128-byte RX FIFO, and the FIFO asserts RFR well
before it fills.

The port is raw by default (no ICANON, no echo) so HCI bytes are not mangled
before bluetoothd calls cfmakeraw(), and the first eight packets in each
direction are printed as `MONDRIAN BTTTY: tx/rx ...` lines: the process that
uses the port cannot log anywhere, so the ring is the read-back channel.

